WEBVTT

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.760
This is Izakaya Nobu.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.490
It's an ordinary izakaya, but 
there is one extraordinary thing.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.990
<b>Nobuyuki Yazawa (32)
 Owner of the izakaya "Nobu"</b>

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.600
Our front door opens into 
a parallel universe!

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.000
<b>Shinobu Senke (23)
 Waitress at the izakaya "Nobu"</b>

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.010
Isekai Izakaya

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.010
Japanese Food From Another World

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:33.010
<b>Beauty and the Abura-age</b>

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:35.660
<i>This is Izakaya Nobu.</i>

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:42.020
<i>It's the place Taishou, Yazawa 
Nobuyuki, and I, Senke Shinobu, run.</i>

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:44.630
<i>I was the daughter of a family who owned 
a traditional Japanese restaurant,</i>

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:46.760
<i>and Taishou was the chef working there.</i>

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.710
<i>I ran away from home because I didn't 
like the partner for the</i>

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:51.020
<i>arranged marriage that 
my parents had set up.</i>

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:55.620
<i>At that time, Taishou was trying to 
open his own place, and so we ended up</i>

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.520
<i>opening this restaurant together.</i>

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.930
<i>And that's how this place came to be.</i>

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:09.550
<b>Izakaya Nobu</b>

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:03.570
<i>This kind of story isn't in and 
of itself anything special,</i>

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:08.490
<i>but there is something very 
different about this restaurant.</i>

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:09.540
<i>And that is...</i>

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:15.350
<b>Three Months Ago</b>

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:21.130
<i>Nobu opens today.</i>

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:24.350
<i>That's why today is special.</i>

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:32.580
<i>I am going to offer up my 
special secret savings.</i>

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:37.720
Please, let our restaurant be successful—

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.200
Why won't the bill leave my fingers?!

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.140
Please... Please watch over Nobu.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.060
Oops, this isn't the time for that.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.160
<i>Nobu's back door opens into 
an alley in Kyoto.</i>

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:07.840
But...

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:09.900
I'm back!

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.300
Nobuyuki-san, can I put 
out the noren curtain?

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:14.550
Please do.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:17.090
Oh, and call me "Taishou."

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.220
Oh, right.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:22.050
Taishou, I'm putting the noren out.

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:22.890
Thank you.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.570
Please watch over us this day.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:45.210
Shinobu-chan, why are you 
running around like that?

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:46.630
Nobuyuki-san!

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:47.740
Call me Taishou.

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:50.970
T-T-Taishou, this city is strange!

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:56.030
<i>It seems like somehow our 
restaurant's front door</i>

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.610
<i>got connected to a parallel universe.</i>

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.470
Wh-What's going on?

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.130
Could this be a dream?

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:05.530
Ouch!

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.490
Guess it's not a dream.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:09.260
Why don't you pinch yourself?

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.140
So if this isn't a dream,

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:15.020
will we even be able to run an izakaya here?

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:16.390
That's what you're thinking about?

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:18.520
Uh, of course.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:22.450
<i>This is how the mysterious izakaya that's 
connected to a parallel universe,</i>

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:24.470
<i>Nobu, opened.</i>

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.020
Thank you!

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.350
Thank you very much.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:34.420
Eva-chan was off today, so 
the whole day was a blur.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.670
I'll put the noren away and 
start the prep for tomorrow.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.530
Hello, girl.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:42.070
Huh?

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:46.170
<b>A Mysterious Woman</b>

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:46.170
Already taking down the noren?

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.090
I'm really sorry!

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:54.230
But we've run out of all 
of our dishes today.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:55.210
What?

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:56.440
You're saying you're out of fish?

00:03:57.890 --> 00:03:59.560
That's really too bad.

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:02.370
Taishou, when did she show up?

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:03.830
I don't know.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:07.370
Oh, you have something!

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:08.330
I'm sorry?

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:09.680
Over there!

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:10.560
I'd like that.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.040
You mean on the home shrine?

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:16.330
That's just some fried tofu.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:17.840
That's it!

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:18.910
That's what I want to eat.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:21.000
I won't leave until I get some.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.360
But we put that out for Oinari-san.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:24.640
So what?

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:28.860
I was just making some fresh abura-age.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:30.350
Why don't I serve you that?

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:35.420
Taishou, you make abura-age 
here in this store?

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:36.240
That's right.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:40.720
It's rather time consuming though, so 
I always make it before you arrive.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:45.990
I wanted to watch my backlog of police 
drama tomorrow, so I was making it now.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:47.590
Really?

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:50.660
But how do you make abura-age anyway?

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:52.240
I don't actually know how you make it.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:58.150
You take thin slices of tofu and, as the 
name suggests, literally fry them.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:02.380
If you remove enough moisture 
from tofu and slowly fry it,

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.610
it gets like this.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:07.600
Wow, it's expanding.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.050
Bubbles form inside it, 
which causes it to swell.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:14.680
The outside gets crispy from 
frying, but when you cool these,

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:17.390
it relaxes and turns into 
the abura-age we all know.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.510
That's a really pretty fox-like color.

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:24.260
And then what do you do with it?

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:29.040
It's still warm, so you could eat it plain.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:32.730
But why don't we grill this?

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:35.100
That will really enhance 
the aroma even further.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:39.440
But just abura-age is a bit boring.

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:41.490
I've got it!

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:43.490
Taro!

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:45.840
Just enough for one person.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.920
They're also delicious grilled.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:54.390
I'm so excited!

00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:01.190
There sure is a strange aura 
about that customer, though.

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:01.900
Yeah.

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:05.720
It's like she just stepped 
out of the Heian Era.

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:09.060
You don't really see clothes 
like that in this world either.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:13.390
I still feel like I've seen 
her somewhere though.

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:14.890
You, too?

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:16.650
I was thinking the same thing.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:18.690
Especially those eyes.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:20.740
It's like I've seen them before.

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:22.020
Oh.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:24.140
<i>Our eyes met.</i>

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:26.960
Yes, I think it's good now.

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:28.290
Is it done?

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:34.810
<b>Grilled Abura-Age and Taro</b>

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:34.810
<b>When grilled on charcoal, 
it's hot and delicious</b>

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.170
Thank you for waiting!

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:32.600
This is grilled abura-age and taro.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:34.800
Please enjoy it with the reishu.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.100
Oh, is that chilled sake?

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:38.480
Wonderful!

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:42.270
Usually it's lukewarm, and sometimes 
there's even some larvae in it.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:42.820
Excuse me?

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:45.140
Oh, I was just talking to myself.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:45.940
Well then...

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:48.710
Wait, there's so little fried tofu.

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:50.600
What happened to the rest of it?

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.460
That's coming up in a moment.

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:55.210
You're plotting something, aren't you?

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:55.870
Not at all.

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:57.410
I'm just cooking.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:00.840
Anyway, I'll try these.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:03.840
<b>Crunch</b>

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:05.470
<b>Crunch</b>

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:09.490
D-Do you like it?

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:12.600
I-It looks like it.

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:17.310
Please peel the taro and 
eat it with the salt.

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:18.970
Let me see.

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:27.860
<b>Cool</b>

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:36.590
It seems this restaurant really knows 
how to make the ingredients shine.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:38.380
Thank you.

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:43.470
And you have obliged a sudden guest.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:46.230
Without our guests, we 
could not be an izakaya,

00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:48.510
so we try to make sure nobody is disappointed.

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:49.880
I see.

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:53.260
And? What will you do with 
the leftover fried tofu?

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:57.340
I will be disappointed if 
it is not served to me.

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:58.430
Yes.

00:07:58.430 --> 00:07:59.890
Please leave it to me.

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:02.390
<b>Nobu's Special 
Surprise Tofu Pocket</b>

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:02.390
<b>Let's see what's inside</b>

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:01.740
Thank you for waiting.

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:09.810
It seems so plain after making me wait.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:12.120
Isn't this just boiled?

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:16.410
Please indulge me and try breaking 
it open with your chopsticks.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:19.910
What's inside there?

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:23.410
<b>Ooze</b>

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.650
It's an egg kinchaku!

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:45.360
The egg does an excellent 
job enveloping the flavor

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.330
of the fried tofu that's 
soaked up plenty of broth.

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:56.510
<b>Cool cool cool cool</b>

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:54.960
And then the refreshing reishu is like 
a cool breeze over the whole landscape.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:56.510
It is truly marvelous.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:58.440
Here is some tea.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:05.370
Thank you very much.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:06.990
That was exquisite.

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:08.690
Thank you very much.

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:11.230
Coming to peek was a good idea.

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:14.690
It seems making this 
connection was worth it.

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:16.950
What did you just say?

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:18.470
Huh?

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:19.590
She's gone.

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:20.830
When did she...

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:26.320
She appeared out of nowhere 
and disappeared just as fast.

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:28.850
I feel like we were tricked by a fox.

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:30.460
I got it.

00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:31.610
What do you mean?

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:34.590
I remembered where
 I've seen her face before.

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:35.670
Really?

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:36.210
Where?

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:38.590
At the Inari Shrine.

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:41.240
That person looked just 
like Oinari-san, you know?

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:46.630
It can't be.

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:50.860
This money looks real.

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:52.990
That bill!

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:57.450
Is something wrong?

00:09:57.970 --> 00:09:59.030
No.

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:02.500
Hey, why don't we put that bill up there?

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:05.470
Good idea.

00:10:08.730 --> 00:10:13.020
<i>She's been watching over us ever since.</i>

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:16.750
<i>Please keep watching over 
Nobu going forward, too.</i>

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:20.760
Let's do our best tomorrow 
again, Shinobu-chan.

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:22.540
Yes!

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:31.290
Next up, we've got Nobu Plus.

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:33.020
<b>Nobu</b>

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.030
<b>Nobu</b>

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:41.650
<b>Takadanobaba</b>

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:41.650
This is Takadanobaba. There are famous 
places here like the "BIG BOX".

00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:46.880
But that was a surprise. Izakaya 
Nobu is connected to Oinari-san?!

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:44.600
<b>Kenichi Nagira</b>

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:48.010
It's a parallel world!

00:10:47.090 --> 00:10:51.070
Well, I feel like I briefly 
saw a part of that mystery.

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:52.470
Oinari-san?

00:10:53.780 --> 00:10:55.370
Hm, Oinari-san.

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:56.840
Let's go out and find that abura-age!

00:10:55.910 --> 00:11:00.430
<b>Narrator
 Teiyu Ichiryusai</b>

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:59.220
Kenichi Nagira is going to go around the bars

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:02.920
here to find the amazing 
dishes featured in "Nobu".

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:02.920
<b>Kenichi Nagira's search for the "Nobu" bar</b>

00:11:02.930 --> 00:11:05.950
First, we pay the shrine a visit.

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:09.020
When you go straight past Kouka, see?

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:10.890
<b>Kenichi Nagira
 Born in Tokyo's Ginza. Made his debut in 1970 as a folk singer.
 Since then, he's been active as an actor, celebrity, essayist.
 Has been in multiple shows that take place in bars or Shitamachi</b>

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:10.920
There it is.

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:11.810
Oh, that's very visible!

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:14.310
The only place in the 
country with tochio-age.

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:15.250
All right, let's go!

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:17.940
This is the only izakaya in all 
of Japan where you can eat

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:19.730
freshly fried tochio-age.

00:11:18.030 --> 00:11:22.770
<b>Takadanobaba    Abura-age</b>

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:22.780
So tochio is that
famous fried tochio?

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:24.440
Yes, it's abura-age from Niigata.

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:25.160
<b>Ageya
Owner  Kenji Katsumata-san</b>

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:25.450
It's very famous!

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:27.790
"Only place in the nation," so 
that means you're the only one?

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:33.510
We get the raw material from a 
shop, well a company, in Niigata,

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:35.170
and then we hand fry them here.

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:38.630
So it's not that they send 
you the fried goods.

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:42.010
We have three flavor variations here.

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:43.950
Then I think I'll try all three.

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:45.280
Thank you very much.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:48.780
Oh, so they really hand fry them one by one.

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:50.790
<b>5 minutes elapsed</b>

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:52.280
Nagira-san, I see you looking at that 
sake, but it'll take another 20 minutes!

00:11:52.280 --> 00:11:56.720
Nagira-san, if you're patient 
here, you'll get your payout!

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:55.990
<b>10 minutes elapsed</b>

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:00.100
See, they are all puffy and fried!

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:00.870
<b>20 minutes elapsed</b>

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:02.180
Is that them?

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:03.880
This is our specialty, the tochio-age.

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:08.620
From the left, grated onion, perilla 
cheese, and browned scallion miso.

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:10.370
If you go in order, the flavors will—

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:11.210
Will harmonize, right?

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:13.920
Eating it in one bite would be a waste. 
These took 20 minutes to make.

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:18.640
First, the fresh taste of grated 
onion. How's the pairing?

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:21.800
Yeah, the crunchy texture of this is great.

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:22.350
Indeed.

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:26.530
They say that things made from rice don't 
fight with things made from beans.

00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:29.910
That's a clever saying.
But how is it?

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:31.480
Not fighting at all.

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:32.830
As usual, you drink well!

00:12:32.830 --> 00:12:34.500
There's melted cheese on top.

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:39.070
Wow, the cheese is so droopy!
 How is it?

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:40.360
Oh, that's totally different.

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:42.470
Usually our female guests 
like that the best.

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:44.460
Women usually like cheese.

00:12:44.750 --> 00:12:48.420
I'm going to just bite right into this one.

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:47.780
Go ahead.

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:50.730
And now, the scallion miso.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:52.380
I bet that's a great match!

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:56.400
Yes, just like the owner said, 
you have go in this order.

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:59.160
If you start with the miso, 
it'll overpower the others.

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:02.090
There are a few different 
theories surrounding abura-age.

00:13:02.090 --> 00:13:05.090
Foxes, who are considered messengers
of the gods, love it...

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:08.490
There are so many stories that it's
hard to get to the bottom of it.

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:12.600
But in the Muromachi era, this 
was a vegetarian monk's dish—

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:16.360
Did you drink this?

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:17.720
No, I didn't.

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:22.080
Are you trying to pin it 
on Oinari-san? Sheesh.

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:19.280
Would you like another?

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:21.040
Yes, please.

